ind the **remainder when twice the number is divided by 4**, i.e., find:\n\n$$\n(2x) \\mod 4\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Use the given congruence\n\nWe know:\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 4 \\pmod{10}\n$$\n\nSo, we can write:\n\n$$\nx = 10k + 4 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n$$\n\nNow compute $ 2x $:\n\n$$\n2x = 2(10k + 4) = 20k + 8\n$$\n\nNow find the remainder when $ 2x $ is divided by 4:\n\n$$\n2x = 20k + 8\n$$\n\nWe can simplify modulo 4:\n\n- $ 20k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4} $, because 20 is divisible by 4.\n- $ 8 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4} $, because 8 is divisible by 4.\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n2x \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{0}\n$$\n\nThe remainder when twice the number is divided by 4 is **0**.", "diagnostic_seconds": 8.770342200063169}
